\pard\pagebb\s1\outlinelevel0\keepn\sb240\sa120\qc\plain\b\fs32 ABSTRACT\par
\pard\plain\sa0\fs12 \par
\pard\plain\qj\sl360\slmult1\sa100\fi720\fs24 Systems built from several language-model agents, for example a researcher, a solver, a verifier and a summariser, can answer harder questions than a single model, but every extra call costs tokens and time. Fixed pipelines call every agent on every task and spend much of that budget on tasks that did not need it. This project asks whether a learned controller can decide, step by step, which agent to call next and when to stop, so that answer quality is maximised after a cost penalty.\par
\pard\plain\qj\sl360\slmult1\sa100\fi720\fs24 The orchestration problem is formulated as a Markov decision process with a 16-number observation, six masked actions and a reward equal to answer quality minus a scaled cost. A Double Deep Q-Network (DDQN) controller is trained in a simulator and compared with a single agent, a fixed pipeline and hand-tuned rule routers on 10,000 unseen tasks, using paired comparisons and decision rules written down before the experiments. The DDQN reaches a mean return of +0.189, against +0.173 for the best rule (+9.3%), +0.153 for a single agent and +0.003 for the fixed pipeline. It keeps its advantage in five of six shifted task distributions and beats the best rule by 15.7% when the difficulty and confidence signals are hidden. Training without the cost penalty collapses (return change of -0.31), which makes the penalty the essential ingredient.\par
\pard\plain\qj\sl360\slmult1\sa100\fi720\fs24 A second study replays recorded outputs of two real models (qwen2.5:3b and gpt-oss-20b) on 50 tasks. A controller trained only in the simulator fails on research questions because it never calls the researcher. Retraining on recorded traces repairs this and ties a tuned rule on the smaller model, but does not beat a rule tuned with task-specific options on the larger one. A live demonstration lets two controllers race on the same task, and a scoreboard over 24 unseen tasks reports wins, ties and losses. The results support learned orchestration as an automatic way to find cost-aware policies, and they show where hand-tuned rules remain competitive.\par
\pard\plain\qj\sl360\slmult1\sa100\fs24 {\b Keywords:} reinforcement learning; Double DQN; multi-agent language model systems; cost-aware orchestration; sim-to-real transfer; pre-registered evaluation.\par
