\pard\pagebb\s1\outlinelevel0\keepn\sb240\sa120\ql\plain\b\fs32 1. INTRODUCTION\par
\pard\s2\outlinelevel1\keepn\sb200\sa120\ql\plain\b\fs26 1.1 Background and Motivation\par
\pard\plain\qj\sl360\slmult1\sa100\fi720\fs24 Applications built on large language models (LLMs) increasingly split one task across specialised agents. A researcher retrieves evidence, a solver drafts an answer, a verifier checks it and a summariser formats it [1], [2], [3]. Such pipelines can raise accuracy, but each call consumes tokens, time and money, and the cost grows with every agent that is added.\par
\pard\plain\qj\sl360\slmult1\sa100\fi720\fs24 Two simple designs dominate practice. A single agent is cheap but cannot recover from its own mistakes. A fixed pipeline calls every agent on every task. Our measurements show that the second is not automatically better. In the simulator of this project the fixed pipeline earns a mean return of +0.003, which is no better than doing nothing, against +0.153 for a single agent, because most tasks do not need all four calls. On recorded outputs of two real models it also scored below the single agent.\par
\pard\plain\qj\sl360\slmult1\sa100\fi720\fs24 The right amount of effort depends on the task, on what has already happened (for example whether a check passed) and on the money left. Choosing the next action from the current situation, with a reward that arrives only at the end, is a sequential decision problem. It is the setting that reinforcement learning (RL) was designed for [4].\par
\pard\s2\outlinelevel1\keepn\sb200\sa120\ql\plain\b\fs26 1.2 Problem Statement\par
\pard\plain\qj\sl360\slmult1\sa100\fi720\fs24 Given a task and a cost budget, choose a sequence of agent calls that ends with STOP, so as to maximise the expected answer quality minus a penalty proportional to the total cost. The controller sees only an observation of the situation (task type, whether an answer exists, the result of the last check, how many calls of each kind were made, and the money and steps left) and not the hidden correctness of the current answer. Chapter 3 gives the formal definition.\par
\pard\s2\outlinelevel1\keepn\sb200\sa120\ql\plain\b\fs26 1.3 Objectives\par
\pard\plain\ls2\ilvl0\fi-360\li720\qj\sl360\slmult1\sa60\fs24 Formulate multi-agent orchestration as a Markov decision process with an explicit reward that is tested against loopholes.\par
\pard\plain\ls2\ilvl0\fi-360\li720\qj\sl360\slmult1\sa60\fs24 Implement a Double DQN controller and fair baselines: a single agent, a fixed pipeline and tuned rule routers.\par
\pard\plain\ls2\ilvl0\fi-360\li720\qj\sl360\slmult1\sa60\fs24 Test, with decision rules written before the runs, whether the learned controller beats the best hand-written rule in a simulator, under distribution shift and when informative signals are hidden.\par
\pard\plain\ls2\ilvl0\fi-360\li720\qj\sl360\slmult1\sa60\fs24 Repeat the comparison on recorded outputs of real language models and measure the gap between simulation and reality.\par
\pard\plain\ls2\ilvl0\fi-360\li720\qj\sl360\slmult1\sa60\fs24 Provide a live demonstration in which two controllers handle the same task side by side.\par
\pard\s2\outlinelevel1\keepn\sb200\sa120\ql\plain\b\fs26 1.4 Contributions\par
\pard\plain\ls1\ilvl0\fi-360\li720\qj\sl360\slmult1\sa60\fs24 A loophole-tested MDP formulation of cost-aware orchestration with action masking and a reward that charges for every call.\par
\pard\plain\ls1\ilvl0\fi-360\li720\qj\sl360\slmult1\sa60\fs24 A simulator study with a documented correction history: three flaws in our own first comparison were found and fixed before any claim was made.\par
\pard\plain\ls1\ilvl0\fi-360\li720\qj\sl360\slmult1\sa60\fs24 A feature study (ablations and a blind variant) showing which observations matter and that the cost penalty is essential.\par
\pard\plain\ls1\ilvl0\fi-360\li720\qj\sl360\slmult1\sa60\fs24 A real-model study on two LLMs using a trace-replay environment, a calibrated cost mapping, held-out tasks and task-level paired statistics.\par
\pard\plain\ls1\ilvl0\fi-360\li720\qj\sl360\slmult1\sa60\fs24 An open, tested code base (87 automated tests) and a live race dashboard in which controllers compete on the same task.\par
\pard\s2\outlinelevel1\keepn\sb200\sa120\ql\plain\b\fs26 1.5 Scope and Limitations\par
\pard\plain\qj\sl360\slmult1\sa100\fi720\fs24 The tasks are single-turn and automatically graded (code with hidden unit tests, numeric problems and fact questions over a small invented document set). The simulator is a stylised model of agent behaviour, and the real-model study uses 50 tasks and two models, so its confidence intervals are wide. A router that asks an LLM to choose the next agent was not built; it is listed as future work. These limits are repeated, with numbers, in Section 5.10. Chapter 2 reviews related work, Chapter 3 defines the problem and system, Chapter 4 the protocol, Chapter 5 the results, and Chapter 6 concludes.\par
\pard\pagebb\s1\outlinelevel0\keepn\sb240\sa120\ql\plain\b\fs32 2. LITERATURE REVIEW AND BACKGROUND\par
\pard\s2\outlinelevel1\keepn\sb200\sa120\ql\plain\b\fs26 2.1 Multi-Agent Language Model Systems\par
\pard\plain\qj\sl360\slmult1\sa100\fi720\fs24 AutoGen [1] lets developers compose applications from conversing agents that can use models, tools and human input. MetaGPT [2] encodes standard operating procedures into prompts and assigns roles to agents in an assembly-line style for software tasks. ReAct [3] interleaves reasoning steps with actions such as search, so that a single model alternates between thinking and acting. Retrieval-augmented generation [5] grounds an answer in retrieved documents, which motivates the researcher agent used here. These systems define what the agents do. The order in which they are called is usually written by hand.\par
\pard\s2\outlinelevel1\keepn\sb200\sa120\ql\plain\b\fs26 2.2 Cost-Aware Use of Language Models\par
\pard\plain\qj\sl360\slmult1\sa100\fi720\fs24 FrugalGPT [6] reduces the cost of using LLM APIs by learning a cascade: a cheap model answers first, and a learned scorer decides whether to accept the answer or escalate to a more expensive model. RouteLLM [7] trains routers that choose between a strong and a weak model for each query, using preference data. Both choose among models. This project chooses among different roles (retrieve, solve, verify, tidy, retry, stop) one step at a time, with a state that includes the result of the previous check and the budget left. That makes the problem sequential, and it is why we model it as an MDP and not as a single routing decision.\par
\pard\s2\outlinelevel1\keepn\sb200\sa120\ql\plain\b\fs26 2.3 Reinforcement Learning Background\par
\pard\plain\qj\sl360\slmult1\sa100\fi720\fs24 A Markov decision process (MDP) is defined by states, actions, transition probabilities and a reward; the agent seeks a policy that maximises the expected discounted return [4]. Q-learning [8] learns the value of each action in each state. The Deep Q-Network (DQN) [9] approximates this function with a neural network and stabilises learning with two devices: an experience replay buffer [10], which breaks the correlation between consecutive samples, and a slowly updated target network. Standard DQN tends to overestimate action values because the same network both selects and evaluates the best next action. Double DQN [11] removes most of this bias by letting the online network select the action and the target network evaluate it. We train with the Adam optimiser [12] and a Huber loss, and we apply action masking, which restricts every maximisation to the actions that are legal in the current state.\par
\pard\s2\outlinelevel1\keepn\sb200\sa120\ql\plain\b\fs26 2.4 Simulation-to-Reality Transfer and Evaluation Practice\par
\pard\plain\qj\sl360\slmult1\sa100\fi720\fs24 Policies trained in simulation often degrade when applied to the real system. Domain randomisation [13] widens the simulator so that reality looks like one more variation. We examine the gap directly by running a simulator-trained controller on recorded real traces and then retraining on those traces. Deep RL results are also sensitive to seeds, hyper-parameters and implementation details [14], and point estimates hide uncertainty [15]. We therefore use several seeds, report final checkpoints without picking the best, give confidence intervals, and compare methods on identical seeded tasks.\par
\pard\s2\outlinelevel1\keepn\sb200\sa120\ql\plain\b\fs26 2.5 Research Gap\par
\pard\plain\qj\sl360\slmult1\sa100\fi720\fs24 Prior work either defines multi-agent systems with hand-written control flow or routes between models once per query. We found no study that learns a sequential controller over heterogeneous roles under an explicit cost penalty, evaluates it against fair tuned baselines with decision rules fixed in advance, and reports how the policy transfers to real models. This report addresses that gap at the scale of a laboratory project.\par
\pard\pagebb\s1\outlinelevel0\keepn\sb240\sa120\ql\plain\b\fs32 3. PROBLEM FORMULATION AND SYSTEM DESIGN\par
\pard\s2\outlinelevel1\keepn\sb200\sa120\ql\plain\b\fs26 3.1 System Overview\par
\pard\plain\qj\sl360\slmult1\sa100\fi720\fs24 A {\b controller} observes the situation and chooses the next action; four {\b agents} (researcher, solver, verifier, summariser) carry it out; an {\b environment} keeps the hidden state, charges the cost of every call and, at the end, grades the answer and pays the reward. The same controller runs on a simulator, on a replay of recorded real-model outputs, and on live model calls (Figure 3.1).\par
