\pard\s2\outlinelevel1\keepn\sb200\sa120\ql\plain\b\fs26 3.2 Tasks and Agents\par
\pard\plain\qj\sl360\slmult1\sa100\fi720\fs24 {\b Coding} tasks ask for a small Python function. {\b Analytical} tasks are word problems with an exact numeric answer. {\b Research} tasks are questions about invented facts in a small document set, which no model can answer from memory. The {\b researcher} retrieves evidence, the {\b solver} drafts an answer, the {\b verifier} returns pass or fail for the current answer (for coding it runs the visible unit tests) and the {\b summariser} formats the final answer.\par
\pard\s2\outlinelevel1\keepn\sb200\sa120\ql\plain\b\fs26 3.3 The Markov Decision Process\par
\pard\plain\qj\sl360\slmult1\sa100\fi720\fs24 The controller sees 16 numbers (Table 3.1). The true difficulty and the true correctness of the current answer are hidden; the difficulty estimate and the confidence score are noisy signals about them. Table 3.2 lists the six actions. Illegal actions are masked in both action selection and the learning update, so the network is never asked to value an action that cannot be taken.\par
