\pard\plain\qj\sl360\slmult1\sa100\fi720\fs24 {\b Reward.} The reward of an episode is R = q x 1[correct] - lambda x C / C_ref - 0.3 x 1[no answer], where C is the sum of the call costs, lambda = 0.5 and C_ref = 0.4. The quality q is 1, except in the simulator where a correct but never summarised answer earns 0.9. The cost is paid at the step where it occurs and the quality at the end, so nothing rewards producing more text. An episode ends at STOP, when the cost reaches the budget (uniform in 0.5 to 1.2), or after 8 steps. Four automated tests check that the reward cannot be gamed: stopping at once scores below solve-then-stop; a useless repeat scores below stopping; a verified answer beats the same answer with wasted calls; and exhausting the budget with no answer scores worst.\par
\pard\s2\outlinelevel1\keepn\sb200\sa120\ql\plain\b\fs26 3.4 The Simulator\par
\pard\plain\qj\sl360\slmult1\sa100\fi720\fs24 One solver call is correct with probability p = skill x (1 - difficulty) x 0.85^n x g(e) x b, where n counts earlier solver calls, g(e) = 0.6 + 0.4e for research and 0.9 + 0.1e for other tasks, e is the evidence level (+0.35 per researcher call, at most 1), and b = 1.10 for a retry after a failed check. The verifier gives the right verdict with the reliability in Table 3.3. Difficulty is Beta(2, 2), the three task types are equally likely, and each cost is multiplied by a random factor in 0.8 to 1.2 (a retry costs 0.8 of a first call).\par
