\pard\plain\qj\sl360\slmult1\sa100\fi720\fs24 {\b Models.} The main model is qwen2.5:3b, run locally through Ollama, which is weak enough to fail often. A stronger check uses openai/gpt-oss-20b through Groq. Research retrieval is a plain keyword search with no model call: an early version let the model summarise the passages, and on irrelevant ones it wrote "none of these help", which the solver copied, so research accuracy fell to 0 of 9 until the researcher was made pure retrieval.\par
\pard\plain\qj\sl360\slmult1\sa100\fi720\fs24 {\b Trace cache and replay.} Agent outputs were recorded once: for each task and evidence level, several solver samples and the verifier verdict for each. Every policy replays the same recordings, so differences come from the policy and not from model luck. A retry replays the next unused sample; the effect of telling the solver why it failed is not recorded (a stated limitation).\par
\pard\plain\qj\sl360\slmult1\sa100\fi720\fs24 {\b Cost mapping.} Real calls are priced as (tokens in + tokens out) / 2500 + 0.02, so that an average solver call costs about 0.12 as in the simulator; the 0.02 covers retrieval, tests and orchestration. This choice fixes what lambda means for real calls.\par
\pard\plain\qj\sl360\slmult1\sa100\fi720\fs24 {\b What the controller sees.} In a pilot, qwen reported confidence 0.98 when right and 1.00 when wrong (36 samples), and no non-oracle difficulty estimate existed. In every real-model experiment the difficulty and confidence entries are therefore set to zero. Section 5.5 first measures what this blindness costs.\par
\pard\s2\outlinelevel1\keepn\sb200\sa120\ql\plain\b\fs26 3.6 The Double DQN Controller\par
\pard\plain\qj\sl360\slmult1\sa100\fi720\fs24 The controller is a network with two hidden layers of 128 units (16 inputs, 6 outputs). Episodes are played with an epsilon-greedy policy over legal actions, and each transition (with the next-state mask) goes into a replay buffer. A mini-batch gives the target y = r + gamma x Q_target(s', argmax over legal a' of Q_online(s', a')), with no future term at the end of an episode. The Huber loss is minimised with Adam (gradient norm clipped at 10), and the target network is refreshed every 1,000 steps. Table 3.5 gives the settings. The blind variant zeroes the difficulty and confidence inputs; the refit variant is trained from scratch on the replay environment built from the development tasks only.\par
