\pard\s2\outlinelevel1\keepn\sb200\sa120\ql\plain\b\fs26 3.7 Baselines and Demonstration System\par
\pard\plain\qj\sl360\slmult1\sa100\fi720\fs24 The baselines are a {\b single agent} (one solver call, then stop), a {\b fixed pipeline} (researcher, solver, verifier, summariser, stop, always), a {\b rule router} (best of 960 hand-built rules over research, verification, retries, a confidence threshold and a give-up difficulty, tuned on 5,000 development tasks), a {\b blind rule} (best of 256 rules that cannot use difficulty or confidence) and, for real traces, a {\b typed rule} (best of 768 rules with per-task-type options). A router in which an LLM picks the next agent was not built. A web application runs any two controllers on the same task, live or from recorded runs, reports a winner using the training reward (computed, never fixed in advance), draws the money spent step by step for both, and offers a scoreboard over the 24 held-out tasks (Section 5.9).\par
\pard\pagebb\s1\outlinelevel0\keepn\sb240\sa120\ql\plain\b\fs32 4. EXPERIMENTAL METHODOLOGY\par
\pard\s2\outlinelevel1\keepn\sb200\sa120\ql\plain\b\fs26 4.1 Splits, Seeds and Statistics\par
\pard\plain\qj\sl360\slmult1\sa100\fi720\fs24 In the simulator, rules are tuned on 5,000 development tasks (seeds 0 to 4,999) and everything is reported on 10,000 test tasks (seeds 100,000 to 109,999); training seeds start at 1,000,000 and never overlap. The shift study uses 5,000 tasks per world. For real models the 50 tasks are split by type, even positions to development (26 tasks: rule tuning and refit) and odd positions to test (24 tasks, 100 seeded episodes each).\par
\pard\plain\qj\sl360\slmult1\sa100\fi720\fs24 Methods are compared on identical seeded tasks, so differences are paired. In the simulator the 95% interval is 1.96 standard errors of the paired difference. For real models the unit is the task: returns are averaged over a task's 100 episodes and the interval is 2.07 times the standard deviation of the 24 paired differences divided by the square root of 24, because episodes of one task are not independent. A method is said to beat another only if the interval excludes zero, and for DDQN results at least four of five seeds must also lie above the comparison.\par
\pard\s2\outlinelevel1\keepn\sb200\sa120\ql\plain\b\fs26 4.2 Pre-Registration and Fairness Controls\par
\pard\plain\qj\sl360\slmult1\sa100\fi720\fs24 Before each experiment the question, settings, predictions and decision rule were written into the decision log and committed to version control; afterwards the log records the result and scores each prediction, including the wrong ones (Appendix C). The initial rule was that learning is pursued only if the optimum beats the best hand rule by at least 10%. Four controls support fairness:\par
\pard\plain\ls1\ilvl0\fi-360\li720\qj\sl360\slmult1\sa60\fs24 {\b Tuning data.} Rules are tuned on development tasks only. Tuning 300 rules on 1,000 tasks once gave a lucky winner (+0.177 development, +0.137 test), so the set was enlarged to 5,000.\par
\pard\plain\ls1\ilvl0\fi-360\li720\qj\sl360\slmult1\sa60\fs24 {\b Strong baselines.} The rule family was extended until it could use every signal the controller uses; a type-specific family was added for real traces.\par
\pard\plain\ls1\ilvl0\fi-360\li720\qj\sl360\slmult1\sa60\fs24 {\b No picking.} Final checkpoints of all five seeds are reported; the demonstration model is seed 0, fixed before any test result was seen.\par
\pard\plain\ls1\ilvl0\fi-360\li720\qj\sl360\slmult1\sa60\fs24 {\b Like for like.} The blind controller is compared with a blind rule.\par
\pard\s2\outlinelevel1\keepn\sb200\sa120\ql\plain\b\fs26 4.3 Implementation\par
\pard\plain\qj\sl360\slmult1\sa100\fi720\fs24 The code is Python with PyTorch: about 3,300 lines in six packages with 87 automated tests. Models run on a personal laptop (qwen2.5:3b through Ollama at about 14 tokens per second; gpt-oss-20b through Groq, capped at 650 requests), and training one simulator controller takes a few minutes. The repository holds the decision log, the logs of every reported run and the recorded traces.\par
\pard\pagebb\s1\outlinelevel0\keepn\sb240\sa120\ql\plain\b\fs32 5. RESULTS AND DISCUSSION\par
\pard\plain\qj\sl360\slmult1\sa100\fi720\fs24 Returns are the reward of Section 3.3, averaged over tasks (higher is better). Simulator numbers use 10,000 unseen test tasks unless stated; real-model numbers use the 24 held-out tasks.\par
\pard\s2\outlinelevel1\keepn\sb200\sa120\ql\plain\b\fs26 5.1 Validity Checks and Corrections\par
\pard\plain\qj\sl360\slmult1\sa100\fi720\fs24 Feasibility tests in simple worlds found the best hand rule within 0 to 1.3% of the optimum, a no-go, so a richer world was designed before it was tested. The first DDQN comparison looked promising (+0.173 on average, 12.7% above the best simple baseline), but three flaws in our own comparison were found before any claim was made: the rule search could not skip the summariser; 300 rules tuned on 1,000 tasks produced a lucky winner (+0.177 development, +0.137 test); and the rules never read the difficulty estimate that the DDQN reads. With difficulty-aware rules (960 rules, 5,000 tuning tasks) the best rule reached +0.173 and the DDQN advantage fell to 0%. The original 12.7% came from a weak baseline.\par
\pard\plain\qj\sl360\slmult1\sa100\fi720\fs24 One mechanism was tested directly. When confidence is below 0.5 the answer is wrong 92% of the time (6,214 cases), which suggested that skipping the check there would help. Replacing the check by an immediate stop changed the return by only +0.0075 (interval 0.0063) and by an immediate retry by -0.0004. The hypothesis was not supported: the check is cheap and the return is flat in that region.\par
\pard\s2\outlinelevel1\keepn\sb200\sa120\ql\plain\b\fs26 5.2 Training Stability\par
\pard\plain\qj\sl360\slmult1\sa100\fi720\fs24 With the original setting (20,000 episodes) the five seeds scored +0.185, +0.183, +0.181, +0.181 and +0.136, so one seed was clearly below the rule. Three longer settings declared in advance were compared on 5,000 development tasks: S1 (60,000 episodes, learning rate 0.001, target update 500) scored +0.181, S2 (learning rate 0.0003, target update 1,000) scored +0.203 and S3 (S2 with exploration over 80% of training) scored +0.198. S2 was chosen on development return alone, and it removed the unstable seed: all five test scores (+0.183 to +0.195) are above the best rule. Figure 5.1 shows its learning curve (mean of five seeds on 1,000 development tasks): the return rises quickly during the first 20,000 episodes and then flattens near +0.205.\par
