\pard\plain\qc\sb120\sa80\b\fs20 Figure 5.4: Loss in return when an ingredient is removed (training world; bar length is the loss).\par
\pard\plain\qj\sl360\slmult1\sa100\fi720\fs24 The cost penalty is by far the most important ingredient: without it the controller learns that calls are free, makes 7.2 calls per task at cost 0.54, and its true return falls to -0.123. Of the signals, the memory of the episode matters most in the training world, because without the last check result the controller stops verifying and behaves like a single agent. The difficulty estimate and the confidence score each cost 0.01 or less because they overlap: both say that the answer is probably wrong. Our predictions were only partly right: the cost-penalty prediction held, the difficulty estimate hurts most among the signals only in the research-heavy world, and the memory of the episode hurt more than expected in the training world.\par
\pard\plain\qj\sl360\slmult1\sa100\fi720\fs24 The blind test removes difficulty and confidence together, because real models give neither (Section 3.5). The best blind rule is the single agent in disguise (+0.153). The blind DDQN reaches +0.177, 15.7% above it, with all five seeds ahead by +0.017 to +0.033, each beyond its interval; the pre-registered bar (at least 3% and at least four of five seeds) was passed. The prediction that the DDQN would lose half of its edge was wrong: it lost 0.012, while the best rule lost 0.020, so blindness hurts the rules more than the learner. The blind rule family is small, which may inflate the gap.\par
\pard\s2\outlinelevel1\keepn\sb200\sa120\ql\plain\b\fs26 5.6 Real Models: What the Recorded Traces Show\par
