\pard\plain\qj\sl360\slmult1\sa100\fi720\fs24 A well-chosen rule beats the fixed pipeline on both models (+0.233 and +0.388), because the pipeline pays about three times the cost of a single agent for steps most tasks do not need. On gpt-oss the refit DDQN beats the single agent (+0.207), and the zero-shot controller is slightly but clearly worse than the single agent (-0.033). The refit DDQN equals the first tuned rule, which could call the researcher only once. When the rule family may be task-specific, the refit DDQN ties the typed rule on qwen (+0.005) and is slightly but clearly behind it on gpt-oss (-0.048): it calls the researcher about 3.3 times on gpt-oss research tasks where two suffice, and one of five seeds scores noticeably lower (+0.656 against +0.79 to +0.81). With 26 training tasks this is unsurprising. On these traces a hand-tuned rule is as good as the learned policy or slightly better, and the learned policy finds a comparable rule without being told which options exist.\par
\pard\s2\outlinelevel1\keepn\sb200\sa120\ql\plain\b\fs26 5.8 The Simulation-to-Reality Gap and Retraining\par
\pard\plain\qj\sl360\slmult1\sa100\fi720\fs24 The simulator-trained blind DDQN, run unchanged on real traces, never calls the researcher on research tasks, so its success there is 0.00 on both models (Table 5.7). In the simulator the researcher only raised the success of later solver calls and was rarely worth its cost; with real models it is indispensable. Retraining on the traces repairs the gap: the refit controller calls the researcher 1.40 (qwen) and 3.33 (gpt-oss) times per research task, and research success becomes 0.46 and 0.87.\par
