\pard\s2\outlinelevel1\keepn\sb200\sa120\ql\plain\b\fs26 5.10 Discussion, Limitations and Threats to Validity\par
\pard\plain\qj\sl360\slmult1\sa100\fi720\fs24 {\b What the evidence supports.} In a simulator whose parameters were fixed before training, a Double DQN finds a cost-aware policy that beats the best hand-written rule by 9.3%, stays ahead under most shifts and beats a blind rule by 15.7%; the cost penalty is the essential ingredient. On real models the choice of agents matters (research needs the researcher; a fixed pipeline wastes money) and the policy retrained on real traces finds a rule as good as a human-tuned one in about one case out of two.\par
\pard\plain\qj\sl360\slmult1\sa100\fi720\fs24 {\b What it does not support.} The learned controller does not clearly beat a well-tuned task-specific rule on real traces, and it does not transfer from the simulator without retraining. Limitations and threats to validity:\par
\pard\plain\ls1\ilvl0\fi-360\li720\qj\sl360\slmult1\sa60\fs24 The real-model study has 24 test tasks, two models and a few recorded samples per setting, so intervals are wide and results show direction, not proof.\par
\pard\plain\ls1\ilvl0\fi-360\li720\qj\sl360\slmult1\sa60\fs24 A retry replays the next recorded sample, so the effect of feedback on the solver is not captured; verifier verdicts are fixed per sample.\par
\pard\plain\ls1\ilvl0\fi-360\li720\qj\sl360\slmult1\sa60\fs24 The token-to-cost conversion (divisor 2,500, overhead 0.02) is a design choice; another choice would change the best policy.\par
\pard\plain\ls1\ilvl0\fi-360\li720\qj\sl360\slmult1\sa60\fs24 The rule families are finite. The typed family was declared after the real results suggested it, and was therefore tuned on development tasks only.\par
\pard\plain\ls1\ilvl0\fi-360\li720\qj\sl360\slmult1\sa60\fs24 The simulator is stylised and its parameters were chosen by the author, not fitted to data; the blind rule family is small.\par
\pard\plain\ls1\ilvl0\fi-360\li720\qj\sl360\slmult1\sa60\fs24 The demonstration model is one training run, and the scoreboard tasks are also the test tasks of the real-model study.\par
\pard\plain\ls1\ilvl0\fi-360\li720\qj\sl360\slmult1\sa60\fs24 Tasks are few and partly invented; retrieval is keyword-based over 16 documents, so research results say nothing about open-domain retrieval.\par
\pard\pagebb\s1\outlinelevel0\keepn\sb240\sa120\ql\plain\b\fs32 6. CONCLUSION AND FUTURE SCOPE\par
\pard\s2\outlinelevel1\keepn\sb200\sa120\ql\plain\b\fs26 6.1 Conclusion\par
\pard\plain\qj\sl360\slmult1\sa100\fi720\fs24 This project formulated the orchestration of a small team of language-model agents as a Markov decision process and trained a Double DQN controller to choose the next agent or to stop. In a simulator, the controller beat every baseline: +0.189 against +0.173 for the best hand-written rule (+9.3%), +0.153 for a single agent and +0.003 for a fixed pipeline. It kept its edge in five of six shifted worlds, and with the difficulty and confidence signals removed it beat the best blind rule by 15.7%. The cost penalty is the essential ingredient of the design: without it the return falls by 0.31.\par
\pard\plain\qj\sl360\slmult1\sa100\fi720\fs24 On recorded outputs of two real models the picture is more modest and more informative. The choice of agents matters, because research questions cannot be answered without the researcher and a fixed pipeline wastes money. A controller trained only in the simulator does not transfer, since it never calls the researcher. Retraining on recorded traces removes that failure, and the retrained controller ties a tuned hand rule on the smaller model, but a rule tuned with task-specific options is slightly better on the larger one. The value of learning is that it discovers a policy of comparable quality automatically and handles the cost trade-off without hand-written rules.\par
\pard\plain\qj\sl360\slmult1\sa100\fi720\fs24 Against the objectives of Section 1.3: the MDP and its loophole tests were built and passed (objective 1); the controller and fair baselines were implemented (objective 2); the simulator hypotheses were tested with decision rules written in advance, including the cases where the predictions were wrong (objective 3); the real-model comparison and the simulation-to-reality gap were measured (objective 4); and the live race with a scoreboard was delivered (objective 5).\par
\pard\s2\outlinelevel1\keepn\sb200\sa120\ql\plain\b\fs26 6.2 Future Scope\par
\pard\plain\ls3\ilvl0\fi-360\li720\qj\sl360\slmult1\sa60\fs24 {\b More tasks and models.} Enlarge the real task set and add models to narrow the intervals and test whether the typed-rule advantage on the larger model is real.\par
\pard\plain\ls3\ilvl0\fi-360\li720\qj\sl360\slmult1\sa60\fs24 {\b Learning from live calls.} Train online on real model calls, and capture the effect of feedback on retries, which the replay environment omits.\par
\pard\plain\ls3\ilvl0\fi-360\li720\qj\sl360\slmult1\sa60\fs24 {\b A more realistic simulator.} Randomise skills, costs and verifier reliability, in the spirit of domain randomisation [13], so that a simulator-trained controller transfers without retraining.\par
\pard\plain\ls3\ilvl0\fi-360\li720\qj\sl360\slmult1\sa60\fs24 {\b A learned difficulty estimate.} Train a cheap predictor of task difficulty so that the controller can use the signal that helped most in the simulator.\par
\pard\plain\ls3\ilvl0\fi-360\li720\qj\sl360\slmult1\sa60\fs24 {\b An LLM router baseline.} Add a baseline in which a language model chooses the next agent, with its own routing calls counted in the cost.\par
\pard\plain\ls3\ilvl0\fi-360\li720\qj\sl360\slmult1\sa60\fs24 {\b Richer reporting.} Use performance profiles and interval estimates across many seeds and tasks, as recommended in [14] and [15].\par
